    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35632": {
      "title": "Academic Initiatives Administrative Coordinator – Office of Graduate Studies - (01490.1)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Academic Initiatives Administrative Coordinator – Office of Graduate Studies - (01490.1) Tweet Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 1/10/2026 Closing Application Date 15/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 19/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES The Academic Initiatives Administrative Coordinator, Office of Graduate Studies, provides pan-institutional coordination of graduate academic administration, governance, faculty participation, awards, and student lifecycle management on behalf of the Office of Graduate Studies. The incumbent coordinates defined academic and administrative processes from initiation through completion, working independently within established policies, procedures, systems, and governance requirements to monitor approvals, milestones, and deadlines; verify procedural compliance; maintain authoritative records; provide procedural guidance; and resolve or escalate process issues. The role also provides senior-level administrative support to the Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies and is accountable for the day-to-day coordination and operation of the Office of Graduate Studies. The incumbent works across Graduate Studies, Faculties and Schools, the Registrar’s Office, Senate Office, Student Awards, Finance, Information Technology, and other units, exercising initiative, sound judgment, and discretion with minimal supervision to address confidential and often sensitive matters. Academic judgments and exceptions remain with the appropriate academic or governance authority; the Coordinator’s role is to ensure that graduate academic processes are consistent, timely, well-documented, data-informed, and policy-aligned. In particular, the incumbent coordinates the Graduate Student Lifecycle Management System, a cross-institutional responsibility for aligning processes, systems, data, milestones, and reporting across the graduate student lifecycle. Working collaboratively with the Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies, the office of graduate studies team and other academic leaders, the incumbent uses highly developed organizational, analytical, and communication skills to support the strategic and operational goals of graduate education at TRU. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Provide day-to-day administrative coordination for the Office of Graduate Studies, ensuring the office operates in an efficient, professional, and service-oriented manner. Manage the Graduate Studies inbox and service function; serve as the initial point of contact for graduate students, faculty, staff, and external contacts, coordinating responses across units and providing procedural guidance; exercise discretion and diplomacy with sensitive or confidential issues, and identify and flag emergent issues to the Vice-Provost in a timely manner. Manage the Vice-Provost’s calendar and correspondence, including scheduling and coordinating meetings, preparing background materials, and anticipating the Vice-Provost’s needs. Manage the Senate Graduate Studies Committee and related governance processes, including submissions, approvals, records, action tracking, and implementation, acting as recording secretary as required; provide similar coordination for other Graduate Studies committees and task forces. Coordinate communications, resources, calendars, and recurring academic requirements for Graduate Program Coordinators across faculties and schools. Oversee graduate faculty participation and appointment processes, including applications, renewals, records, approvals, and expiry monitoring. Oversee graduate awards administration, including eligibility review, adjudication, communications, deadlines, and records, liaising with Student Awards and Financial Aid and academic units. Manage graduate supervision and supervisory committee administrative processes and records. Coordinate the development, implementation, and ongoing administration of a Graduate Student Lifecycle Software Management System, supporting consistent tracking of students from admission and registration through supervision, progression, completion, and graduation, in partnership with the Registrar’s Office, IT Services, and faculties. Support the development, implementation, and administration of academic initiatives in graduate education, including new and revised graduate program proposals, curriculum and policy changes, and program reviews or quality-assurance processes, working closely with faculties, Senate, and the Office of the Provost. Oversee graduate policies, procedures, forms, records, and reporting, and lead related process-improvement work in collaboration with academic and administrative units across TRU. Monitor and track the Office of Graduate Studies’ operating budget, including processing expense claims, requisitions, and procurement, reconciling transactions, and preparing budget and financial summaries for the Vice-Provost. Research, compile, and draft reports, briefing notes, correspondence, and presentation materials on graduate studies matters, including institutional and provincial or national reporting requirements. Maintain the Office of Graduate Studies’ authoritative records, files, and databases, including keeping content on the Graduate Studies website and SharePoint site accurate and current. Support hiring, onboarding, and personnel administration processes for the Office of Graduate Studies. Undertake special projects and assignments delegated by the Vice-Provost, bringing them to completion with minimal supervision. Perform other related duties as required. REPORTS TO Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ATTRIBUTES This position requires a high degree of initiative and meticulous attention to detail. The incumbent must possess a professional attitude, maturity, diplomacy, discretion, and confidentiality, along with strong organizational and analytical skills. A university degree in a relevant discipline with a minimum of 3-5 years of related experience, or a post-secondary diploma in a related field with a minimum of 5-10 years of related administrative experience supporting a senior academic leader; experience in graduate studies or academic administration is strongly preferred. Sound understanding of university governance, policies, and procedures, including experience supporting or coordinating governance bodies (e.g., Senate or Senate committees). Familiarity with graduate studies structures and processes across the student lifecycle (admissions, supervision, progression, milestones such as thesis/dissertation defense, and graduation). Self-motivated, highly organized, and resourceful, with a demonstrated ability to work independently within established policies and procedures, exercising sound judgment while recognizing when a matter requires escalation to academic or governance authority. Demonstrated ability to work tactfully and courteously with a diverse range of people, from senior academic administrators to graduate students, faculty, and staff, as well as external partners and agencies. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a strong ability to draft clear, concise, and appropriately toned correspondence, reports, and briefing materials. Demonstrated ability to multi-task and manage competing priorities and deadlines across multiple concurrent processes in a fast-paced environment. Excellent time-management, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated ability to plan, coordinate, and bring delegated projects to completion with minimal supervision, including cross-unit initiatives involving multiple stakeholders. Experience coordinating or implementing administrative information systems or process/records management tools is an asset (e.g., a student lifecycle management system). Excellent computer literacy, including word processing, spreadsheets, databases, and presentation software; experience with website or SharePoint content management is an asset. Experience with budget monitoring, financial tracking, and procurement processes. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise discretion in handling sensitive academic and personnel matters. Pay Band $66,609.00 - $76,125.00 ( Admin Pay Band AH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.4 Last sync: 2026-10-02 06:25:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 1/10/2026 Closing Application Date 15/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 19/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES The Academic Initiatives Administrative Coordinator, Office of Graduate Studies, provides pan-institutional coordination of graduate academic administration, governance, faculty participation, awards, and student lifecycle management on behalf of the Office of Graduate Studies. The incumbent coordinates defined academic and administrative processes from initiation through completion, working independently within established policies, procedures, systems, and governance requirements to monitor approvals, milestones, and deadlines; verify procedural compliance; maintain authoritative records; provide procedural guidance; and resolve or escalate process issues. The role also provides senior-level administrative support to the Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies and is accountable for the day-to-day coordination and operation of the Office of Graduate Studies. The incumbent works across Graduate Studies, Faculties and Schools, the Registrar’s Office, Senate Office, Student Awards, Finance, Information Technology, and other units, exercising initiative, sound judgment, and discretion with minimal supervision to address confidential and often sensitive matters. Academic judgments and exceptions remain with the appropriate academic or governance authority; the Coordinator’s role is to ensure that graduate academic processes are consistent, timely, well-documented, data-informed, and policy-aligned. In particular, the incumbent coordinates the Graduate Student Lifecycle Management System, a cross-institutional responsibility for aligning processes, systems, data, milestones, and reporting across the graduate student lifecycle. Working collaboratively with the Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies, the office of graduate studies team and other academic leaders, the incumbent uses highly developed organizational, analytical, and communication skills to support the strategic and operational goals of graduate education at TRU. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Provide day-to-day administrative coordination for the Office of Graduate Studies, ensuring the office operates in an efficient, professional, and service-oriented manner. Manage the Graduate Studies inbox and service function; serve as the initial point of contact for graduate students, faculty, staff, and external contacts, coordinating responses across units and providing procedural guidance; exercise discretion and diplomacy with sensitive or confidential issues, and identify and flag emergent issues to the Vice-Provost in a timely manner. Manage the Vice-Provost’s calendar and correspondence, including scheduling and coordinating meetings, preparing background materials, and anticipating the Vice-Provost’s needs. Manage the Senate Graduate Studies Committee and related governance processes, including submissions, approvals, records, action tracking, and implementation, acting as recording secretary as required; provide similar coordination for other Graduate Studies committees and task forces. Coordinate communications, resources, calendars, and recurring academic requirements for Graduate Program Coordinators across faculties and schools. Oversee graduate faculty participation and appointment processes, including applications, renewals, records, approvals, and expiry monitoring. Oversee graduate awards administration, including eligibility review, adjudication, communications, deadlines, and records, liaising with Student Awards and Financial Aid and academic units. Manage graduate supervision and supervisory committee administrative processes and records. Coordinate the development, implementation, and ongoing administration of a Graduate Student Lifecycle Software Management System, supporting consistent tracking of students from admission and registration through supervision, progression, completion, and graduation, in partnership with the Registrar’s Office, IT Services, and faculties. Support the development, implementation, and administration of academic initiatives in graduate education, including new and revised graduate program proposals, curriculum and policy changes, and program reviews or quality-assurance processes, working closely with faculties, Senate, and the Office of the Provost. Oversee graduate policies, procedures, forms, records, and reporting, and lead related process-improvement work in collaboration with academic and administrative units across TRU. Monitor and track the Office of Graduate Studies’ operating budget, including processing expense claims, requisitions, and procurement, reconciling transactions, and preparing budget and financial summaries for the Vice-Provost. Research, compile, and draft reports, briefing notes, correspondence, and presentation materials on graduate studies matters, including institutional and provincial or national reporting requirements. Maintain the Office of Graduate Studies’ authoritative records, files, and databases, including keeping content on the Graduate Studies website and SharePoint site accurate and current. Support hiring, onboarding, and personnel administration processes for the Office of Graduate Studies. Undertake special projects and assignments delegated by the Vice-Provost, bringing them to completion with minimal supervision. Perform other related duties as required. REPORTS TO Vice-Provost, Graduate Studies QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS/ATTRIBUTES This position requires a high degree of initiative and meticulous attention to detail. The incumbent must possess a professional attitude, maturity, diplomacy, discretion, and confidentiality, along with strong organizational and analytical skills. A university degree in a relevant discipline with a minimum of 3-5 years of related experience, or a post-secondary diploma in a related field with a minimum of 5-10 years of related administrative experience supporting a senior academic leader; experience in graduate studies or academic administration is strongly preferred. Sound understanding of university governance, policies, and procedures, including experience supporting or coordinating governance bodies (e.g., Senate or Senate committees). Familiarity with graduate studies structures and processes across the student lifecycle (admissions, supervision, progression, milestones such as thesis/dissertation defense, and graduation). Self-motivated, highly organized, and resourceful, with a demonstrated ability to work independently within established policies and procedures, exercising sound judgment while recognizing when a matter requires escalation to academic or governance authority. Demonstrated ability to work tactfully and courteously with a diverse range of people, from senior academic administrators to graduate students, faculty, and staff, as well as external partners and agencies. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with a strong ability to draft clear, concise, and appropriately toned correspondence, reports, and briefing materials. Demonstrated ability to multi-task and manage competing priorities and deadlines across multiple concurrent processes in a fast-paced environment. Excellent time-management, organizational, and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated ability to plan, coordinate, and bring delegated projects to completion with minimal supervision, including cross-unit initiatives involving multiple stakeholders. Experience coordinating or implementing administrative information systems or process/records management tools is an asset (e.g., a student lifecycle management system). Excellent computer literacy, including word processing, spreadsheets, databases, and presentation software; experience with website or SharePoint content management is an asset. Experience with budget monitoring, financial tracking, and procurement processes. Ability to maintain confidentiality and exercise discretion in handling sensitive academic and personnel matters. Pay Band $66,609.00 - $76,125.00 ( Admin Pay Band AH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
