    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35626": {
      "title": "Sessional - Faculty (Academic Developer) - (02050.12019)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details This site attempts to protect users against Cross-Site Request Forgeries attacks. In order to do so, you must have JavaScript enabled in your web browser otherwise this site will fail to work correctly for you.\n\t See details of your web browser for how to enable JavaScript. Sessional - Faculty (Academic Developer) - (02050.12019) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 6/10/2026 Closing Application Date 21/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits), Instructional Support Anticipated Start Date 20/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 31/7/2027 DUTIES OVERVIEW Thompson Rivers University, in partnership with the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT), is delivering a select cohort of the Master of Education program to members of the St’uxwtews (Bonaparte First Nation) and NVIT employees beginning Fall 2026. TRU is seeking an experienced academic developer to provide peer mentorship for instructors teaching in the program. This role involves working alongside the course instructors to Indigenize and decolonize Master of Education course materials, teaching practices, and assessments, as well as support effective delivery of a blended learning format. This is a highly collaborative role, requiring the Academic Developer to work closely with the TRU MEd Faculty Liaison, MEd Program Co-Coordinators, and NVIT and St’uxwtews representatives. Some travel to the NVIT campus in Merritt, BC, may be required. RESPONSIBILITIES Thompson Rivers University is seeking a sessional faculty member to serve as the Academic Developer for the St’uxwtews and NVIT MEd Cohorts. The Academic Developer is responsible for assisting course instructors with designing culturally responsive curriculum that meets the Senate-approved course outlines while honouring Indigenous and decolonial epistemologies and pedagogies. One position is available for the first three semesters of the MEd program from September 1, 2026 through July 31, 2027. The Academic Developer will receive 1-credit per semester for a total of three credits. Responsibilities include: To actively support faculty in Indigenizing and decolonizing the MEd curriculum and pedagogical practices through formal and informal individual and group consultations. To act as a resource for teaching and learning, including curriculum development and alignment, and assessment of student learning. To assist in ensuring MEd curriculum and instructional practices are in compliance with Senate and University policies. To support faculty in designing engaging, accessible, and culturally responsive online learning experiences. To support faculty in the ethical and pedagogically appropriate use of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in teaching, learning, and assessment, consistent with University policies and principles of academic integrity. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD or EdD is required. A Bachelor of Education degree is required. Demonstrated successful post-secondary teaching in a teacher education program is required. Demonstrated expertise in faculty learning and development (academic development) and experience working in a postsecondary Teaching and Learning Centre is required. Self-identification as an Indigenous person (e.g., First Nations, Inuit, or Métis) and demonstrated knowledge of Indigenous worldviews, histories, cultures, contemporary realities, and experience building respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, as well as experience working in community is required. Demonstrated experience in integrating Indigenous knowledge and teaching methods is required (TRC CA 62); and, teaching excellence, including developing culturally appropriate curricula is required (TRC CA 10). Proven skills in building student capacity for intercultural understanding, empathy, and mutual respect is required (TRC CA 63). Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Faculty of Education and Social Work -> Masters of Education Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.4 Last sync: 2026-10-08 20:10:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Faculty (Sessional) Posting In effect from 6/10/2026 Closing Application Date 21/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Instructional Type Academic/Career(max 24 credits), Instructional Support Anticipated Start Date 20/10/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) 31/7/2027 DUTIES OVERVIEW Thompson Rivers University, in partnership with the Nicola Valley Institute of Technology (NVIT), is delivering a select cohort of the Master of Education program to members of the St’uxwtews (Bonaparte First Nation) and NVIT employees beginning Fall 2026. TRU is seeking an experienced academic developer to provide peer mentorship for instructors teaching in the program. This role involves working alongside the course instructors to Indigenize and decolonize Master of Education course materials, teaching practices, and assessments, as well as support effective delivery of a blended learning format. This is a highly collaborative role, requiring the Academic Developer to work closely with the TRU MEd Faculty Liaison, MEd Program Co-Coordinators, and NVIT and St’uxwtews representatives. Some travel to the NVIT campus in Merritt, BC, may be required. RESPONSIBILITIES Thompson Rivers University is seeking a sessional faculty member to serve as the Academic Developer for the St’uxwtews and NVIT MEd Cohorts. The Academic Developer is responsible for assisting course instructors with designing culturally responsive curriculum that meets the Senate-approved course outlines while honouring Indigenous and decolonial epistemologies and pedagogies. One position is available for the first three semesters of the MEd program from September 1, 2026 through July 31, 2027. The Academic Developer will receive 1-credit per semester for a total of three credits. Responsibilities include: To actively support faculty in Indigenizing and decolonizing the MEd curriculum and pedagogical practices through formal and informal individual and group consultations. To act as a resource for teaching and learning, including curriculum development and alignment, and assessment of student learning. To assist in ensuring MEd curriculum and instructional practices are in compliance with Senate and University policies. To support faculty in designing engaging, accessible, and culturally responsive online learning experiences. To support faculty in the ethical and pedagogically appropriate use of generative artificial intelligence (GenAI) in teaching, learning, and assessment, consistent with University policies and principles of academic integrity. QUALIFICATIONS A PhD or EdD is required. A Bachelor of Education degree is required. Demonstrated successful post-secondary teaching in a teacher education program is required. Demonstrated expertise in faculty learning and development (academic development) and experience working in a postsecondary Teaching and Learning Centre is required. Self-identification as an Indigenous person (e.g., First Nations, Inuit, or Métis) and demonstrated knowledge of Indigenous worldviews, histories, cultures, contemporary realities, and experience building respectful, reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, as well as experience working in community is required. Demonstrated experience in integrating Indigenous knowledge and teaching methods is required (TRC CA 62); and, teaching excellence, including developing culturally appropriate curricula is required (TRC CA 10). Proven skills in building student capacity for intercultural understanding, empathy, and mutual respect is required (TRC CA 63). Pay Band $2,732.34 - $2,732.34 ( Sessional Faculty - Academic (per credit) ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Faculty of Education and Social Work -> Masters of Education Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
    },
    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35611": {
      "title": "Open Learning Faculty Member (Web): CONV 1061: Event Marketing - (03027.638)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Open Learning Faculty Member (Web): CONV 1061: Event Marketing - (03027.638) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Open Learning Faculty Member Posting In effect from 6/10/2026 Closing Application Date 12/10/2026 OLFM Type Regular Continuous Teaching Allocation Percentage (TAP) 50% Anticipated Start Date 2/11/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES This course reviews the concepts and tools used to design and implement a successful event marketing strategy. The focus of the course is on applying contemporary principles of strategic marketing to the process of event management. These concepts are applicable to the broadest definition of the event management industry including festivals, sporting events, community celebrations, cultural events and arts productions. A student-defined case study further defines the application of course content. Open Learning Faculty Members are teaching staff in the Open Learning division of TRU, and the role of the Open Learning Faculty Member is to actively encourage, support and guide students toward success in their academic studies.  Open Learning Faculty Members must be skilled in teaching, fully knowledgeable of the subject matter and demonstrate a positive, open and caring attitude in all interaction with students. Responsibilities of the Open Learning Faculty Member (Web) include but are not limited to: Administrative set up and record keeping Initiating and maintaining regular contact with the student as specified in the collective agreement Responding to all student queries by phone, e-mail or other method as appropriate within the time frame outlined in the collective agreement. Marking all assignments, posting results, and providing prompt feedback Marking exams and posting results We anticipate 15 - 20 students in this course per year. These numbers are only estimates and actual numbers for the course may be greater or less than the estimated numbers. QUALIFICATIONS We are looking for candidates with a Master’s Degree in a relevant subject area with applicable industry experience and significant recent academic experience in the subject area, along with documented excellence in helping students achieve their goals. In addition to excellent verbal and written communication skills, the preferred candidates will have demonstrated skills and abilities in distance education. The Open Learning Faculty Member must: Be knowledgeable and experienced in the subject matter of the course Have received training or have successful experience in the active facilitation of learning in a collaborative online environment Have direct experience working with current educational technologies, tools and Learning Management Systems Have demonstrated knowledge of effective pedagogy in the use of current educational technology and tools Have demonstrated skills and abilities in the delivery of flexible learning materials, the application of instructional technology and in the application of adult learning principles The pay for Open Learning Faculty Members varies based on several factors including the number of students in the course, the type of work, the type of course and the number of assessments completed by students in the course.  The total pay includes amounts based on hourly rates as well as set dollar amounts for certain types of work.  For more information, please refer to the TRU/TRUOLFA Collective Agreement: https://truolfa.ca/assets/documents/Collective Agreement-2022-2025- TRU-TRUOLFA.pdf Pay Band Location BC CA (Primary) Qualified TRUOLFA applicants will be given first consideration filling 