    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35628": {
      "title": "Associate Director, AI and Automation - (01492.1)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Associate Director, AI and Automation - (01492.1) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 29/9/2026 Closing Application Date 14/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/11/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES The Associate Director, AI & Automation leads the implementation of artificial intelligence and process automation adoption for Thompson Rivers University. Reporting to the CIO and serving as a key advisor, this role supports TRU's AI and automation portfolio: setting strategic direction and governance standards, adoption review, directing the Power Team initiative and future programs and building the team, partnerships, and institutional capability needed to scale responsible AI use across the university. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Leadership & Governance Support TRU's institution-wide AI and automation strategy, setting direction, priorities, and success metrics in alignment with the university's strategic plan. Adjudicating risk classification and review of proposed AI and automation use cases (e.g., under the Safe Start framework). Serve as a key advisor to the CIO on AI strategy, opportunity, and risk; hold a standing voice in ITS leadership team. Set institutional standards and policy guardrails for responsible AI and automation adoption. Direct and mentor a growing team in the design, governance, and deployment of AI and automation solutions. Maintain working command of MLOps principles (model versioning, monitoring, and deployment) to guide the team's technical practice. Strategic Planning & Program Delivery Develop and execute the multi-year roadmap for integrating AI and RPA into administrative and academic-support workflows, with Power Team as the flagship program. Identify, prioritize, and business-case new automation and AI opportunities based on time, accuracy, consistency, and risk impact. Set and track program-level goals (time saved, error reduction, adoption, satisfaction) and report progress against a defined roadmap, not just individual pilots. Own budget planning and vendor/contract management for the AI and automation portfolio, including delegated authority for AI tool and automation procurement decisions. Team Leadership & Resourcing Build, structure, and grow the AI & Automation team, with accountability for hiring, performance management, and career development. Define team structure and resourcing plans that scale with institutional demand, including a stated growth trajectory as the portfolio expands. Training & Capability Building Direct the design and delivery of training and enablement programs for administrative leaders and support staff on AI and automation tools. Foster a culture of curiosity, experimentation, and responsible use across the institution. Institutional & External Engagement Represent TRU externally on AI and automation practice, including engagement with the BC/national peer community, sector consultations, and conference or panel participation. Build and maintain relationships with administrative leaders, ITS teams, AI/automation vendors, and industry experts. Contributes to the institutional narrative around AI and automation, building stakeholder trust and sustained momentum through regular communication of progress and outcomes. Feedback, Metrics & Continuous Improvement Establish a portfolio-level measurement framework and report efficiency gains, adoption rates, risk posture, and challenges to senior leadership on a regular cadence. Collect and act on feedback from training sessions and pilot/program implementations to refine strategy, tools, and governance. Hold the team accountable for outcomes, documenting both successes and lessons learned to inform the next phase of the roadmap. REPORTS TO Chief Information Officer (CIO) QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in information technology, Computer Science, Business Administration, or a related field required; Master's degree in a related field strongly preferred and weighted favorably. At least five (5) years of leadership experience, ideally within a post-secondary or complex institutional setting, including experience building or scaling a team. At least three (3) years of hands-on experience in software development, scripting, or automation engineering, with a strong foundation in coding best practices. Demonstrated experience developing institutional policy, governance frameworks, or risk/adoption review processes — not solely technical delivery. Experience managing vendor relationships, contracts, and budget for a technology program or portfolio. Strong understanding of AI and RPA technologies and their institutional applications; prior experience building AI/ML platforms. Familiarity with cloud AI/ML platforms such as AWS SageMaker, Azure Machine Learning, or equivalent. Familiarity with organizational change management methodologies; experience leading change management and stakeholder engagement at an institutional level. Excellent leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills, including experience representing an organization externally (peer institutions, sector bodies, conferences). Ability to analyze data and feedback to drive continuous improvement across a program portfolio. Full-time position with occasional travel for stakeholder meetings, sector engagements, and training sessions. Collaborative work environment with a defined path for professional development and portfolio/team growth. Pay Band $111,375.00 - $124,959.00 ( Admin Pay Band CH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Information Technology Services -> AVP, IT Services Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.4 Last sync: 2026-10-08 20:10:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 29/9/2026 Closing Application Date 14/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/11/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES The Associate Director, AI & Automation leads the implementation of artificial intelligence and process automation adoption for Thompson Rivers University. Reporting to the CIO and serving as a key advisor, this role supports TRU's AI and automation portfolio: setting strategic direction and governance standards, adoption review, directing the Power Team initiative and future programs and building the team, partnerships, and institutional capability needed to scale responsible AI use across the university. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Strategic Leadership & Governance Support TRU's institution-wide AI and automation strategy, setting direction, priorities, and success metrics in alignment with the university's strategic plan. Adjudicating risk classification and review of proposed AI and automation use cases (e.g., under the Safe Start framework). Serve as a key advisor to the CIO on AI strategy, opportunity, and risk; hold a standing voice in ITS leadership team. Set institutional standards and policy guardrails for responsible AI and automation adoption. Direct and mentor a growing team in the design, governance, and deployment of AI and automation solutions. Maintain working command of MLOps principles (model versioning, monitoring, and deployment) to guide the team's technical practice. Strategic Planning & Program Delivery Develop and execute the multi-year roadmap for integrating AI and RPA into administrative and academic-support workflows, with Power Team as the flagship program. Identify, prioritize, and business-case new automation and AI opportunities based on time, accuracy, consistency, and risk impact. Set and track program-level goals (time saved, error reduction, adoption, satisfaction) and report progress against a defined roadmap, not just individual pilots. Own budget planning and vendor/contract management for the AI and automation portfolio, including delegated authority for AI tool and automation procurement decisions. Team Leadership & Resourcing Build, structure, and grow the AI & Automation team, with accountability for hiring, performance management, and career development. Define team structure and resourcing plans that scale with institutional demand, including a stated growth trajectory as the portfolio expands. Training & Capability Building Direct the design and delivery of training and enablement programs for administrative leaders and support staff on AI and automation tools. Foster a culture of curiosity, experimentation, and responsible use across the institution. Institutional & External Engagement Represent TRU externally on AI and automation practice, including engagement with the BC/national peer community, sector consultations, and conference or panel participation. Build and maintain relationships with administrative leaders, ITS teams, AI/automation vendors, and industry experts. Contributes to the institutional narrative around AI and automation, building stakeholder trust and sustained momentum through regular communication of progress and outcomes. Feedback, Metrics & Continuous Improvement Establish a portfolio-level measurement framework and report efficiency gains, adoption rates, risk posture, and challenges to senior leadership on a regular cadence. Collect and act on feedback from training sessions and pilot/program implementations to refine strategy, tools, and governance. Hold the team accountable for outcomes, documenting both successes and lessons learned to inform the next phase of the roadmap. REPORTS TO Chief Information Officer (CIO) QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in information technology, Computer Science, Business Administration, or a related field required; Master's degree in a related field strongly preferred and weighted favorably. At least five (5) years of leadership experience, ideally within a post-secondary or complex institutional setting, including experience building or scaling a team. At least three (3) years of hands-on experience in software development, scripting, or automation engineering, with a strong foundation in coding best practices. Demonstrated experience developing institutional policy, governance frameworks, or risk/adoption review processes — not solely technical delivery. Experience managing vendor relationships, contracts, and budget for a technology program or portfolio. Strong understanding of AI and RPA technologies and their institutional applications; prior experience building AI/ML platforms. Familiarity with cloud AI/ML platforms such as AWS SageMaker, Azure Machine Learning, or equivalent. Familiarity with organizational change management methodologies; experience leading change management and stakeholder engagement at an institutional level. Excellent leadership, communication, and interpersonal skills, including experience representing an organization externally (peer institutions, sector bodies, conferences). Ability to analyze data and feedback to drive continuous improvement across a program portfolio. Full-time position with occasional travel for stakeholder meetings, sector engagements, and training sessions. Collaborative work environment with a defined path for professional development and portfolio/team growth. Pay Band $111,375.00 - $124,959.00 ( Admin Pay Band CH ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Information Technology Services -> AVP, IT Services Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
    },
    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35702": {
      "title": "People and Culture Consultant - (01071.23)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details This site attempts to protect users against Cross-Site Request Forgeries attacks. In order to do so, you must have JavaScript enabled in your web browser otherwise this site will fail to work correctly for you.\n\t See details of your web browser for how to enable JavaScript. People and Culture Consultant - (01071.23) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 14/10/2026 Closing Application Date 28/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/12/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES As the main point of contact, the People and Culture Consultant provides comprehensive strategic and operational advice, guidance, and support to Managers, Deans, Directors and VPs and their respective departments and faculties across campus, in all facets of Human Resources and Labour Relations. Reporting to the Executive Director of HR Strategic Partnerships, the Consultant collaborates on developing and implementing best practice programs, policies, and systems to support the University's leaders and managers. This role delivers high-level service and expertise in Human Resource management across various domains. The Consultant's areas of responsibility encompass recruitment and selection, employee and labour relations, and conducting preliminary inquiries into matters such as harassment, discrimination, professional misconduct, and conflicts of interest. Additionally, the role involves coaching managers on performance management, fostering organizational development, advising on support staff job positions and faculty salary placements. Through these diverse functions, the Consultant provides comprehensive HR support to enhance the University's operational effectiveness. The key to the success of the People and Culture Consultant in attaining their intended outcomes is their ability to develop strategic partnerships, understanding the key challenges of the departments and/or faculties and providing coaching and strategic advice based on HR best practices within their assigned portfolios. The People and Culture Consultant will operate with considerable independence, while exercising discretion, and initiative. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Provide knowledge and strategic advice to portfolio leaders on employee and labour relations.  Provide guidance and support in the interpretation and application of three collective agreements, the Benefits and Working Conditions of Administrative Employees and HR policies. Works with managers and union representatives to discuss issues and find both conventional and creative ways to resolve problems, considering past practices, best practices, and the mutual interest of all stakeholders while maintaining the integrity of the relevant collective agreement. Advise and coach portfolio leaders on performance management issues and ensure consistent application of corrective actions in line with applicable collective agreements and institutional practices. Leads step 1 grievance meetings and advises and coaches managers through the Grievance process while recommending effective remedies. Provides input to and participates in resolution meetings and arbitrations to resolve labour management issues. May participate in collective bargaining including input and research towards preparation of bargaining proposals. Works collaboratively with internal client groups to champion the development and the application of TRU’s employment brand and recruitment and selection strategy. Ensure processes are aligned with TRU’s strategic goals, underlying TRU as an “Employer of Choice” for potential applicants and existing employees. Work collaboratively with portfolio leaders to develop work force plans that meet the organization’s short- and long-term resource needs. Coaches department leaders on recruitment and selection best practices and ensures compliance with collective agreements, procedures, and working conditions, connecting leaders with organizational resources. Advise and coach portfolio leaders on effective performance and development planning and support the process by providing relevant training and practical tools. Conducts preliminary inquiries into complaints of harassment, conflict of interest, professional misconduct, discrimination and abuse, and reporting to the Responsible Officer as required. Based on the findings, advises and supports leaders in appropriate action to mitigate and respond to complaints including discipline outcomes and best course(s) of action, while ensuring consistency across the organization and compliance with relevant collective agreements and policy. In collaboration with the Organizational Learning & Engagement team, promote and initiate employee development programs to support TRU’s strategic goals, departmental goals and enhance employee skills. Supports the development and delivery of relevant training and initiatives that support a culture of learning and build organizational capability and capacity. Ensures consistency and internal equity across the organization related to compensation and salary administration. Administers compensation programs and advises organizational leaders on consistent and equitable application. Advises portfolio leaders on the CUPE Joint Job Evaluation program and works with HR employees to implement changes and communicate outcomes. Oversees the development of support staff job descriptions and administrative role profiles. Provides guidance and coaching to organizational leaders in the Administrative job evaluation process. Research, develop and manage HR projects that focus on the implementation of Human Resources best practices and further TRU’s organizational goals as identified an approved. Acts as a change agent in partnership with organizational leaders to implement new practices, programs, tools and organizational and departmental changes that are aligned with TRU’s strategic goals. Facilitates transactions such as approvals for recruitment and hiring using the HRIS and reviews and approves confidential hiring packages based on established standards. Coach and support HR Officers in their day-to-day operational transactions and responsibilities. Research and collaborate with external third parties. Responsible for building and managing effective relationships with a wide variety of internal and external stakeholders. Participate in and lead various campus committees (ie: Disability Management, Joint Employee/Union committees, etc). REPORTS TO Executive Director, HR Strategic Partnerships QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS An undergraduate degree in an area of HR and/or Business Minimum four years of experience demonstrating active involvement leading and solving complex HR issues Experience working with unions is an asset A professional designation (CPHR) is preferred A passion to exemplify client service and delivering on commitments to a high standard Proven ability in building collaborative working relationships with employees at all levels, including union representatives Ability to act as a change agent and resolve conflict while achieving optimal outcomes Proven ability to conduct inquiry meetings and demonstrate investigative skills Strong interpersonal, problem solving, and decision-making abilities Demonstrated knowledge and application of relevant laws and regulations: labour, workplace health and safety, human rights, and employment standards Ability to provide sound professional advice and direction for all aspects of Human Resources to all levels within the organization Ability to identify clients’ needs and provide advice, guidance and planning related to a variety of initiatives that support strategic directions Proven ability to work as an integral member of a highly functioning human resources team to deliver high quality services to the University’s communities Excellent verbal and written skills Strong computer knowledge (Microsoft Office Suite) Demonstrated experience in delivering presentations to large and small groups. Ability to mentor and coordinate the work of other HR staff Pay Band $99,151.00 - $111,375.00 ( Admin Pay Band CL ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> People and Culture Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.5 Last sync: 2026-10-16 19:00:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 14/10/2026 Closing Application Date 28/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/12/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES As the main point of contact, the People and Culture Consultant provides comprehensive strategic and operational advice, guidance, and support to Managers, Deans, Directors and VPs and their respective departments and faculties across campus, in all facets of Human Resources and Labour Relations. Reporting to the Executive Director of HR Strategic Partnerships, the Consultant collaborates on developing and implementing best practice programs, policies, and systems to support the University's leaders and managers. This role delivers high-level service and expertise in Human Resource management across various domains. The Consultant's areas of responsibility encompass recruitment and selection, employee and labour relations, and conducting preliminary inquiries into matters such as harassment, discrimination, professional misconduct, and conflicts of interest. Additionally, the role involves coaching managers on performance management, fostering organizational development, advising on support staff job positions and faculty salary placements. Through these diverse functions, the Consultant provides comprehensive HR support to enhance the University's operational effectiveness. The key to the success of the People and Culture Consultant in attaining their intended outcomes is their ability to develop strategic partnerships, understanding the key challenges of the departments and/or faculties and providing coaching and strategic advice based on HR best practices within their assigned portfolios. The People and Culture Consultant will operate with considerable independence, while exercising discretion, and initiative. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Provide knowledge and strategic advice to portfolio leaders on employee and labour relations.  Provide guidance and support in the interpretation and application of three collective agreements, the Benefits and Working Conditions of Administrative Employees and HR policies. Works with managers and union representatives to discuss issues and find both conventional and creative ways to resolve problems, considering past practices, best practices, and the mutual interest of all stakeholders while maintaining the integrity of the relevant collective agreement. Advise and coach portfolio leaders on performance management issues and ensure consistent application of corrective actions in line with applicable collective agreements and institutional practices. Leads step 1 grievance meetings and advises and coaches managers through the Grievance process while recommending effective remedies. Provides input to and participates in resolution meetings and arbitrations to resolve labour management issues. May participate in collective bargaining including input and research towards preparation of bargaining proposals. Works collaboratively with internal client groups to champion the development and the application of TRU’s employment brand and recruitment and selection strategy. Ensure processes are aligned with TRU’s strategic goals, underlying TRU as an “Employer of Choice” for potential applicants and existing employees. Work collaboratively with portfolio leaders to develop work force plans that meet the organization’s short- and long-term resource needs. Coaches department leaders on recruitment and selection best practices and ensures compliance with collective agreements, procedures, and working conditions, connecting leaders with organizational resources. Advise and coach portfolio leaders on effective performance and development planning and support the process by providing relevant training and practical tools. Conducts preliminary inquiries into complaints of harassment, conflict of interest, professional misconduct, discrimination and abuse, and reporting to the Responsible Officer as required. Based on the findings, advises and supports leaders in appropriate action to mitigate and respond to complaints including discipline outcomes and best course(s) of action, while ensuring consistency across the organization and compliance with relevant collective agreements and policy. In collaboration with the Organizational Learning & Engagement team, promote and initiate employee development programs to support TRU’s strategic goals, departmental goals and enhance employee skills. Supports the development and delivery of relevant training and initiatives that support a culture of learning and build organizational capability and capacity. Ensures consistency and internal equity across the organization related to compensation and salary administration. Administers compensation programs and advises organizational leaders on consistent and equitable application. Advises portfolio leaders on the CUPE Joint Job Evaluation program and works with HR employees to implement changes and communicate outcomes. Oversees the development of support staff job descriptions and administrative role profiles. Provides guidance and coaching to organizational leaders in the Administrative job evaluation process. Research, develop and manage HR projects that focus on the implementation of Human Resources best practices and further TRU’s organizational goals as identified an approved. Acts as a change agent in partnership with organizational leaders to implement new practices, programs, tools and organizational and departmental changes that are aligned with TRU’s strategic goals. Facilitates transactions such as approvals for recruitment and hiring using the HRIS and reviews and approves confidential hiring packages based on established standards. Coach and support HR Officers in their day-to-day operational transactions and responsibilities. Research and collaborate with external third parties. Responsible for building and managing effective relationships with a wide variety of internal and external stakeholders. Participate in and lead various campus committees (ie: Disability Management, Joint Employee/Union committees, etc). REPORTS TO Executive Director, HR Strategic Partnerships QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS An undergraduate degree in an area of HR and/or Business Minimum four years of experience demonstrating active involvement leading and solving complex HR issues Experience working with unions is an asset A professional designation (CPHR) is preferred A passion to exemplify client service and delivering on commitments to a high standard Proven ability in building collaborative working relationships with employees at all levels, including union representatives Ability to act as a change agent and resolve conflict while achieving optimal outcomes Proven ability to conduct inquiry meetings and demonstrate investigative skills Strong interpersonal, problem solving, and decision-making abilities Demonstrated knowledge and application of relevant laws and regulations: labour, workplace health and safety, human rights, and employment standards Ability to provide sound professional advice and direction for all aspects of Human Resources to all levels within the organization Ability to identify clients’ needs and provide advice, guidance and planning related to a variety of initiatives that support strategic directions Proven ability to work as an integral member of a highly functioning human resources team to deliver high quality services to the University’s communities Excellent verbal and written skills Strong computer knowledge (Microsoft Office Suite) Demonstrated experience in delivering presentations to large and small groups. Ability to mentor and coordinate the work of other HR staff Pay Band $99,151.00 - $111,375.00 ( Admin Pay Band CL ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> People and Culture Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
