    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35691": {
      "title": "Assistant Dean, Faculty of Law - (01168.11)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Assistant Dean, Faculty of Law - (01168.11) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 13/10/2026 Closing Application Date 26/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/12/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Under the general direction and leadership of the Dean, and consistent with the TRU Faculty of Law’s vision and mission as well as applicable university and other regulatory and contractual requirements, to work collaboratively as a key member of the Faculty’s leadership and administrative team in planning for and managing the business of the Law School in a sustainable, fiscally prudent, transparent, coordinated, inclusive, respectful, and welcoming manner, in support of achieving the Law School’s strategic and operational objectives. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Administration: The Assistant Dean is a senior non-academic position in the Faculty of Law. Reporting to the Dean, and working with the university administration, the Assistant Dean is responsible to: Review/develop and implement all administrative components necessary for the Faculty of Law; Provide strategic oversight for events and provide leadership to the Events Coordinator in the planning, coordination, and execution of faculty events; Develop and implement business plans for the Law School’s initiatives designed to enhance legal education and increase ties with the general public; Coordinate Law School communication strategies, including the design and production of newsletters, research reports, etc.; Oversees the faculty’s website, ensuring its content remains current, relevant, and complete. May assists at student recruitment events and assists the Dean in the recruitment of staff; Serve as an active member of various committees, contributing to the confidential evaluation, adjudication, and selection of candidates for admission, awards, bursaries, and externships; and Provide leadership, guidance, and support to students regarding academic concerns, examination and course-related matters, and navigating the law school experience. Regulatory issues and compliance: Working with the Dean and in consultation, as her delegate, with the TRU senior administration and others as appropriate, the Assistant Dean is responsible to: Ensure the Law School continues to meet the requirements of the Federation of Law Societies of Canada and other regulatory or funding agencies, including through the provision of annual and other periodic financial, project activity and competency reports; Identify and work toward changes in TRU Senate regulations and policies necessary for the effective and legally-compliant operation of the Law School; Develop Faculty of Law regulations that are consistent with TRU Senate regulations; and Provide other strategic advice to the Dean as required. In carrying out this work, the Assistant Dean will take direction from the Dean, with active input from others as appropriate. Enhancement of legal education: Working with faculty members and others as appropriate, and subject to their approval/consensus, the Assistant Dean is responsible for identifying opportunities and potential resources for helping to develop and implement internal systems and initiatives to: Provide academic support as needed as well as extracurricular enhancement of law students’ educational experience at TRU; Assist in the development of the internship and externship programs; and Along with the Director of Career Services and the Justice in Residence, working to provide increased opportunities for law students’ interaction with members of the legal profession and the judiciary. In carrying out this work, the Assistant Dean takes direction from and works closely with the Dean, who has primary responsibility for all teaching allocations, course approvals, student services and other academic matters. Outreach: In support of the Dean, the Assistant Dean is responsible for helping to identify opportunities, contacts and initiatives to: Enhance the profile, stature and reputation for excellence of the Law School, its faculty and its alumni with the legal, judicial, academic and business communities locally, provincially, nationally and internationally; Lead in advancement and fundraising opportunities for the Law School, from various sources and through activities at local, provincial and national levels, including money raised for specific initiatives or events; Lead the development and submission of grant applications and ensure the timely completion of required grant reports and related documentation; Actively build the TRU Law community, drawing upon the local bar and bench and also including lawyers and others from elsewhere in the province and across Canada; and Work with the Director of Career Services to enhance the career opportunities of alumni and the reputation of TRU in that regard. REPORTS TO Dean, Faculty of Law QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS This position requires the following qualifications: Undergraduate Bachelor’s Degree plus LLB/JD, MBA, MPA or equivalent postgraduate degree relevant to the responsibilities of this position; Working knowledge of relevant statutes, regulations and law; At least five years relevant experience, preferably in the post-secondary system, a law firm, as general corporate counsel or in a government department, including: Responsibility for supervision of staff and/or work; Playing a significant role in the development of major new initiatives; Clinical experience is highly recommended; Demonstrated success at project planning and issues and change management; Demonstrated success in team-building, team leadership and influence management; Extensive knowledge of and contacts within the B.C. and Canadian legal community; Effective oral and written communication and interpersonal skills; Tact, discretion and excellent legal and strategic judgment; Computer literacy, including familiarity with spreadsheets and data analysis; Strong organizational and problem solving skills; Demonstrated ability to work both cooperatively with others and independently; Demonstrated ability to manage projects by setting and meeting realistic goals, timelines, milestones and performance measures; Demonstrated ability to work in a manner consistent with general direction; and Availability to work evenings and/or weekends as required Pay Band $124,959.00 - $139,899.00 ( Admin Pay Band DL ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Faculty of Law -> Faculty of Law Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.5 Last sync: 2026-10-16 19:00:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 13/10/2026 Closing Application Date 26/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 1/12/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Under the general direction and leadership of the Dean, and consistent with the TRU Faculty of Law’s vision and mission as well as applicable university and other regulatory and contractual requirements, to work collaboratively as a key member of the Faculty’s leadership and administrative team in planning for and managing the business of the Law School in a sustainable, fiscally prudent, transparent, coordinated, inclusive, respectful, and welcoming manner, in support of achieving the Law School’s strategic and operational objectives. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Administration: The Assistant Dean is a senior non-academic position in the Faculty of Law. Reporting to the Dean, and working with the university administration, the Assistant Dean is responsible to: Review/develop and implement all administrative components necessary for the Faculty of Law; Provide strategic oversight for events and provide leadership to the Events Coordinator in the planning, coordination, and execution of faculty events; Develop and implement business plans for the Law School’s initiatives designed to enhance legal education and increase ties with the general public; Coordinate Law School communication strategies, including the design and production of newsletters, research reports, etc.; Oversees the faculty’s website, ensuring its content remains current, relevant, and complete. May assists at student recruitment events and assists the Dean in the recruitment of staff; Serve as an active member of various committees, contributing to the confidential evaluation, adjudication, and selection of candidates for admission, awards, bursaries, and externships; and Provide leadership, guidance, and support to students regarding academic concerns, examination and course-related matters, and navigating the law school experience. Regulatory issues and compliance: Working with the Dean and in consultation, as her delegate, with the TRU senior administration and others as appropriate, the Assistant Dean is responsible to: Ensure the Law School continues to meet the requirements of the Federation of Law Societies of Canada and other regulatory or funding agencies, including through the provision of annual and other periodic financial, project activity and competency reports; Identify and work toward changes in TRU Senate regulations and policies necessary for the effective and legally-compliant operation of the Law School; Develop Faculty of Law regulations that are consistent with TRU Senate regulations; and Provide other strategic advice to the Dean as required. In carrying out this work, the Assistant Dean will take direction from the Dean, with active input from others as appropriate. Enhancement of legal education: Working with faculty members and others as appropriate, and subject to their approval/consensus, the Assistant Dean is responsible for identifying opportunities and potential resources for helping to develop and implement internal systems and initiatives to: Provide academic support as needed as well as extracurricular enhancement of law students’ educational experience at TRU; Assist in the development of the internship and externship programs; and Along with the Director of Career Services and the Justice in Residence, working to provide increased opportunities for law students’ interaction with members of the legal profession and the judiciary. In carrying out this work, the Assistant Dean takes direction from and works closely with the Dean, who has primary responsibility for all teaching allocations, course approvals, student services and other academic matters. Outreach: In support of the Dean, the Assistant Dean is responsible for helping to identify opportunities, contacts and initiatives to: Enhance the profile, stature and reputation for excellence of the Law School, its faculty and its alumni with the legal, judicial, academic and business communities locally, provincially, nationally and internationally; Lead in advancement and fundraising opportunities for the Law School, from various sources and through activities at local, provincial and national levels, including money raised for specific initiatives or events; Lead the development and submission of grant applications and ensure the timely completion of required grant reports and related documentation; Actively build the TRU Law community, drawing upon the local bar and bench and also including lawyers and others from elsewhere in the province and across Canada; and Work with the Director of Career Services to enhance the career opportunities of alumni and the reputation of TRU in that regard. REPORTS TO Dean, Faculty of Law QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS This position requires the following qualifications: Undergraduate Bachelor’s Degree plus LLB/JD, MBA, MPA or equivalent postgraduate degree relevant to the responsibilities of this position; Working knowledge of relevant statutes, regulations and law; At least five years relevant experience, preferably in the post-secondary system, a law firm, as general corporate counsel or in a government department, including: Responsibility for supervision of staff and/or work; Playing a significant role in the development of major new initiatives; Clinical experience is highly recommended; Demonstrated success at project planning and issues and change management; Demonstrated success in team-building, team leadership and influence management; Extensive knowledge of and contacts within the B.C. and Canadian legal community; Effective oral and written communication and interpersonal skills; Tact, discretion and excellent legal and strategic judgment; Computer literacy, including familiarity with spreadsheets and data analysis; Strong organizational and problem solving skills; Demonstrated ability to work both cooperatively with others and independently; Demonstrated ability to manage projects by setting and meeting realistic goals, timelines, milestones and performance measures; Demonstrated ability to work in a manner consistent with general direction; and Availability to work evenings and/or weekends as required Pay Band $124,959.00 - $139,899.00 ( Admin Pay Band DL ) Location Kamloops, BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Faculty of Law -> Faculty of Law Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
    },
    "https://tru.hua.hrsmart.com/hr/ats/Posting/view/35642": {
      "title": "Manager, Presidential Initiatives & Engagement - (01493.1)",
      "description": "Jump to main content Attention Safari users: This site requires cookies to function. Please click here to accept the cookie. Cookies will be used strictly for the functioning of the site. Toggle navigation Job Search View All Jobs Create Account Log in Help Help on this Page Job Details Manager, Presidential Initiatives & Engagement - (01493.1) Share this job as a link in your status update to LinkedIn. Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 13/10/2026 Closing Application Date 26/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 16/11/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Reporting to the Chief of Staff, the Manager, Presidential Initiatives & Engagement advances priority initiatives of the Office of the President and supports the President’s strategic engagement with internal and external communities. The Manager leads and coordinates assigned presidential initiatives from planning through implementation, bringing together people and resources from across the university to achieve institutional objectives. The position develops project plans, coordinates stakeholders, monitors commitments and deliverables, identifies emerging issues, and ensures effective follow-through on priorities established by the President and Chief of Staff. The Manager also supports the President’s engagement with students, faculty, staff, Indigenous communities, government, community organizations, partners and other stakeholders. Working closely with colleagues across the university, the position helps ensure presidential engagements are purposeful, well-coordinated and aligned with institutional priorities. Operating in a dynamic executive environment, the Manager exercises considerable initiative, judgment, discretion and political acuity and develops effective working relationships across organizational boundaries. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Presidential Initiatives and Project Management Leads and coordinates strategic initiatives and special projects assigned by the President or Chief of Staff. Develops project scopes, objectives, work plans, timelines, milestones and deliverables. Coordinates cross-functional teams and working groups involving academic and administrative units across the university. Monitors progress against agreed objectives and identifies risks, dependencies and emerging issues requiring attention. Tracks decisions, commitments and action items and ensures appropriate follow-through. Conducts research, environmental scans and analysis to support presidential initiatives and institutional decision-making. Prepares project updates, briefing materials, presentations, recommendations and other materials for the President and Chief of Staff. Establishes appropriate measures of success and evaluates the effectiveness and outcomes of assigned initiatives. Supports task forces, advisory groups and other time-limited initiatives established by the President or Chief of Staff. Presidential Engagement Supports the planning and implementation of the President’s strategic engagement with university and external communities. Works with the Chief of Staff and other university offices to identify engagement opportunities that advance institutional priorities and strengthen key relationships. Develops engagement plans for priority stakeholders, communities and initiatives. Coordinates presidential visits, roundtables, consultations, community engagements and other strategic activities. Works with relevant university units to ensure the President is appropriately briefed and prepared for significant engagements. Tracks issues, commitments and opportunities arising from presidential engagements and coordinates appropriate institutional follow-up. Develops and maintains effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders on behalf of the President’s Office. Coordination and Institutional Relationships Serves as a point of coordination between the President’s Office and faculties, schools, administrative units and other areas of the university on assigned initiatives. Builds collaborative relationships across organizational boundaries and facilitates effective participation in presidential initiatives. Works closely with Communications, Government Relations, Advancement, Indigenous Relations, academic leadership and other areas as appropriate to ensure coordinated institutional approaches. Supports effective information flow between the President’s Office and university leadership on assigned priorities. Research, Briefing and Communications Researches and analyzes institutional and external issues relevant to presidential priorities. Synthesizes complex information into concise briefing notes, decision documents, presentations and recommendations. Drafts correspondence, speaking points, background materials and other executive-level materials as required. Works with communications colleagues to ensure that messaging associated with presidential initiatives and engagements is coordinated and consistent with institutional priorities. Maintains appropriate records and documentation related to presidential initiatives, decisions and commitments. Office of the President Handles sensitive and confidential matters with discretion and sound judgment. Undertakes other strategic projects and responsibilities assigned by the Chief of Staff. REPORTS TO Chief of Staff, Office of the President and Vice-Chancellor QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS Undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline; a graduate degree or equivalent combination of education and experience would be an asset. Several years of progressively responsible experience in project management, stakeholder engagement, executive administration, public affairs, communications, government relations, higher education or a related environment. Demonstrated experience managing complex projects involving multiple stakeholders and competing priorities. Strong relationship-building and stakeholder-engagement skills. Excellent written communication skills, including demonstrated ability to prepare concise executive-level briefing and decision materials. Strong research, analytical and problem-solving capabilities. Demonstrated ability to exercise discretion, diplomacy, political acuity and sound judgment. Ability to work effectively with senior leaders and with diverse internal and external communities. Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Knowledge of the post-secondary sector and the British Columbia public-sector environment would be an asset. Pay Band $99,151.00 - $111,375.00 ( Admin Pay Band CL ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Office of President and Vice-Chancellor Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job v18.5.5 Last sync: 2026-10-16 19:00:04 Application Restrictions Open to both Internal and external Job Type Administrative/Management Posting In effect from 13/10/2026 Closing Application Date 26/10/2026 Applications will be reviewed commencing the closing application date and may continue until the position is filled. Full/Part Time Full Time Admin Type Ongoing Anticipated Start Date 16/11/2026 Position End Date (If Applicable) DUTIES Reporting to the Chief of Staff, the Manager, Presidential Initiatives & Engagement advances priority initiatives of the Office of the President and supports the President’s strategic engagement with internal and external communities. The Manager leads and coordinates assigned presidential initiatives from planning through implementation, bringing together people and resources from across the university to achieve institutional objectives. The position develops project plans, coordinates stakeholders, monitors commitments and deliverables, identifies emerging issues, and ensures effective follow-through on priorities established by the President and Chief of Staff. The Manager also supports the President’s engagement with students, faculty, staff, Indigenous communities, government, community organizations, partners and other stakeholders. Working closely with colleagues across the university, the position helps ensure presidential engagements are purposeful, well-coordinated and aligned with institutional priorities. Operating in a dynamic executive environment, the Manager exercises considerable initiative, judgment, discretion and political acuity and develops effective working relationships across organizational boundaries. MAJOR RESPONSIBILITIES Presidential Initiatives and Project Management Leads and coordinates strategic initiatives and special projects assigned by the President or Chief of Staff. Develops project scopes, objectives, work plans, timelines, milestones and deliverables. Coordinates cross-functional teams and working groups involving academic and administrative units across the university. Monitors progress against agreed objectives and identifies risks, dependencies and emerging issues requiring attention. Tracks decisions, commitments and action items and ensures appropriate follow-through. Conducts research, environmental scans and analysis to support presidential initiatives and institutional decision-making. Prepares project updates, briefing materials, presentations, recommendations and other materials for the President and Chief of Staff. Establishes appropriate measures of success and evaluates the effectiveness and outcomes of assigned initiatives. Supports task forces, advisory groups and other time-limited initiatives established by the President or Chief of Staff. Presidential Engagement Supports the planning and implementation of the President’s strategic engagement with university and external communities. Works with the Chief of Staff and other university offices to identify engagement opportunities that advance institutional priorities and strengthen key relationships. Develops engagement plans for priority stakeholders, communities and initiatives. Coordinates presidential visits, roundtables, consultations, community engagements and other strategic activities. Works with relevant university units to ensure the President is appropriately briefed and prepared for significant engagements. Tracks issues, commitments and opportunities arising from presidential engagements and coordinates appropriate institutional follow-up. Develops and maintains effective relationships with internal and external stakeholders on behalf of the President’s Office. Coordination and Institutional Relationships Serves as a point of coordination between the President’s Office and faculties, schools, administrative units and other areas of the university on assigned initiatives. Builds collaborative relationships across organizational boundaries and facilitates effective participation in presidential initiatives. Works closely with Communications, Government Relations, Advancement, Indigenous Relations, academic leadership and other areas as appropriate to ensure coordinated institutional approaches. Supports effective information flow between the President’s Office and university leadership on assigned priorities. Research, Briefing and Communications Researches and analyzes institutional and external issues relevant to presidential priorities. Synthesizes complex information into concise briefing notes, decision documents, presentations and recommendations. Drafts correspondence, speaking points, background materials and other executive-level materials as required. Works with communications colleagues to ensure that messaging associated with presidential initiatives and engagements is coordinated and consistent with institutional priorities. Maintains appropriate records and documentation related to presidential initiatives, decisions and commitments. Office of the President Handles sensitive and confidential matters with discretion and sound judgment. Undertakes other strategic projects and responsibilities assigned by the Chief of Staff. REPORTS TO Chief of Staff, Office of the President and Vice-Chancellor QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED KNOWLEDGE/SKILLS Undergraduate degree in a relevant discipline; a graduate degree or equivalent combination of education and experience would be an asset. Several years of progressively responsible experience in project management, stakeholder engagement, executive administration, public affairs, communications, government relations, higher education or a related environment. Demonstrated experience managing complex projects involving multiple stakeholders and competing priorities. Strong relationship-building and stakeholder-engagement skills. Excellent written communication skills, including demonstrated ability to prepare concise executive-level briefing and decision materials. Strong research, analytical and problem-solving capabilities. Demonstrated ability to exercise discretion, diplomacy, political acuity and sound judgment. Ability to work effectively with senior leaders and with diverse internal and external communities. Strong organizational skills and demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Knowledge of the post-secondary sector and the British Columbia public-sector environment would be an asset. Pay Band $99,151.00 - $111,375.00 ( Admin Pay Band CL ) Location BC CA (Primary) # of Hires Needed 1 Organizational Unit Thompson Rivers University -> Office of President and Vice-Chancellor Diversity and Inclusion Commitment: Thompson Rivers University is strongly committed to hiring based on merit with a focus on fostering diversity of thought within our community. We welcome those who would contribute to the further diversification of our staff, our faculty and its scholarship including, but not limited to, women, Indigenous, Black and People of Colour, persons with disabilities and persons of any sexual orientation or gender identity. Please note that all qualified candidates are encouraged to apply, however applications from Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. NOTE: To learn more about Thompson Rivers University or living in Kamloops or Williams Lake please visit our Careers website . External Candidates invited to interview will be required to bring a copy of their transcripts to the interview. Apply to this Job"
